1. The run-time engine
----------------------
The game program is what runs your games and is what the end player will see.
To start the character moving use the arrow keys, to stop him press the same
arrow key again. DO NOT HOLD DOWN THE ARROW KEY. To switch mode (from walk ->
look -> interact -> talk -> use) click the RIGHT button, to use the current
mode on something click the LEFT button over it. You can also move the mouse
to the top of the screen and choose the mode from the bar which appears.
NOTE: Pressing the arrow keys when not in walk mode will move the cursor,
not the man.
Press TAB to view your inventory, Ctrl-P to pause game, Ctrl-Q to quit game.
To use an object, click on the bag on the top bar, then click on the
inventory item you want to use. To save your current game position either
press F5 or use the title bar, to load press F7.
1.1 THE DEMO GAME
Once you've read through the tutorial, or even before, why not try out the
demo game. It's not particularly difficult to complete, and the graphics
aren't much good (I'm no artist), but it does show you how all the different
options work. Once you've played the demo, you should have a pretty good idea
of how the Adventure Creator works and what it can do.

2. What do I do? - Tutorial
---------------------------
First of all you need to think of a plot and draw the screen where the
character will begin the adventure in your favourite paint program. You must
save it as a 320x200 256-colour VGA picture in the PCX or BMP format.
You may think that 320x200 is a bit blocky but it is the only non-SVGA mode
that can do 256 colours, and it requires less memory than most other
graphics modes.

2.0 THE PROJECT MANAGER
The Project Manager is a new addition to the Adventure Creator which I just
wrote, so the rest of this documentation hasn't been updated to include it.
What it provides is an easy way to edit the project options, launch the
screen creator and compile the game. Type PROJMAN project_name to start
the Project Manager.

2.1 SETTING UP A SCREEN
Once done, load the screen creator by typing ROOMMAKE. You will be presented
with a blank screen with a menu bar at the top. To open a menu, move the
cursor over the title and drag down, DO NOT CLICK ON THE MENU BAR. Move the
cursor until it highlights the option you want and click the LEFT button.

Click on Load scene... on the File menu. Throughout this .doc file, that
will be known as  File | Load scene... . A dialog box will appear with all
the PCX files in the current directory. It doesn't allow you to change
drive or directory so you should make sure all your PCX files are in the
same directory. You can click on the up and down arrows to scroll the list.
When you see your background scene, click on it. It will be loaded and
displayed on the screen.

NOTE: The ROOMMAKE program has on-line context-sensitive help. Just press F1
      or choose Misc | Help Contents.
NOTE: Although there is a Start New option on the File menu, it is
      recommended that to start a new screen you quit to DOS and then
      type ROOMMAKE again. The Start New option isn't very reliable.

2.2 WALLS
Now you can put the walls in. These are barriers that the character's feet
will not be allowed to cross. So, for example, if you have a fence in your
picture, place a wall along the bottom of it. To place a wall click on
Walls | Add  or press A. Then click the mouse at the beginning and end of
the wall. Repeat this for everywhere you need to stop the player walking.
If the wall isn't horizontal or vertical it's probably best to place another
wall alongside it 1 pixel away. This stops the player wriggling through
by pressing combinations of arrow keys.

2.3 WALK-BEHIND AREAS
Once you've got all the walls in, you can define the walk-behind areas.
These are areas that will be drawn in front of the character. For example,
if you had a pillar on the screen, the pillar is a walk-behind area because
if the character is behind it then that part of them won't be drawn. To add
a walk-behind area click on  Walk-behind | Add  or press O. You now have to
draw round the outline of the object. Click the left button to start a line,
then the RIGHT button to finish it. Repeat this process until you have an
enclosed shape, but click the LEFT button to finish the last line. Then click
somewhere inside the shape, and it will be filled in blue. If you didn't
enclose it, the whole screen will fill blue; start again.
You now need to define the BASE - that is, the level at which the character's
feet must be for him to walk behind it. Obviously, you don't want him shown
behind it if he's standing in front! Choose  Walk-behind | Base  and click
at the bottom pixel of the shape. A blue horizontal line will appear. Once
you have positioned it correctly using the LEFT button, click the RIGHT button
to fix it there.
To create a second walk-behind area click  Walk-Behind | Change  or press C.
The background colour of the Walk-behind menu will change to green. Now repeat
the process for this walk-behind area. Note that you can only have one BASE
per colour, so if you have two objects at the same level, you can use the
same colour for both. There is a maximum of 15 walk-behind areas per screen.
Choosing  Walk-behind | Change  on the 15th will go back to 1 (dark blue).

2.4 SAVING THE SCREEN
Now would be a good time to save your work so far. Choose  File | Save... .
A dialog box similar to the load scene one will appear. Click where it says
"*.CRM" and when the cursor appears type in the name you want to call the file.
The actual screens used by the game are ROOM1.CRM, ROOM2.CRM, etc so to
test your screen you will have to give it the name of the correct room. The
screen where the character starts is ROOM1.CRM, or screen 1.
If you want to test your screen, just call it ROOM1.CRM, then see section 2.8
for info on how to load it into the run-time engine. Move the character around
with the arrow keys. Check that he can't walk through anything he shouldn't be
able to and that he walks behind the right areas. Then quit by pressing CTRL-Q
and load ROOMMAKE once more. Load your screen back in by clicking
File | Load...  and clicking on your screen.
NOTE: The AC program will check for a sub-directory called ROOMS and use
any *.CRM files in it, however this is not compulsory.

2.5 CONDITIONS
The penultimate thing to do is to define the CONDITIONS: what happens to the
character and the course of the game when various events happen. For example,
suppose your background scene had a signpost on it. You want the player to
be able to look at the signpost. To do this, choose  Condition | Set screen
and do the same process as for walk-behind areas (see above): draw the
outline of the signpost and fill it in. Once you've done this you need to
specify what happens when you look at it. You'll notice that the background
colour of the Condition menu is dark blue: this is type 1. If you cycle
through the condition areas by choosing  Condition | Select  or pressing N,
you will notice that, as with walk-behind areas, the menu colour changes.
If you press N once, the colour will go dark green: this is type 2. Press it
again, turquoise: type 3, and so on. There is a limit of 15 condition areas
per screen.
Now, click on  Condition | Change . The display will switch to text mode and
a list will appear. There are the misellaneous conditions at the top - they
don't relate to objects or condition areas. They include events like walking
off the screen. Below them is a list of events which start with Look at type 1
and go down to Speak to object 9. Use the down arrow key to move the + to the
line which says "Look at type 1" and press return. A menu will appear. It is
asking what should happen when you click the eye on the signpost. Scroll the
menu to "Display message %d" and press return. A cursor will appear next to
the number 0. Press return to accept it (the numbering of messages starts
at 0).
Now, press ESCAPE to return to the interface and choose  Condition | Messages.
(SHORTCUT: You can also hit F10 when the condition is selected in the
condition list).
The display will switch to text-mode once more, and the screen will be blank
save for the top line. Press A to create message 0, and press RETURN.
Type in the message you want displayed when someone looks at the signpost.
Press RETURN to end the message. To force a carriage return when displaying,
type a "[" in the message, for example, type
It's a signpost which reads [ WARNING!
This is not necessary at the end of every line: text wrapping does occur,
however you might want to insert a carriage return somewhere yourself.
Note that if you press F2 (while not typing in a message), you can toggle
"Display Next Message" on and off. If this is on, then the next message will
be displayed after the player has clicked to make this one disappear. This is
useful for long messages and conversations between characters. In fact,
using F3 you can tell the program to display the message as speech instead of
in the standard box in the middle of the screen.
2.5.1 GIVING POINTS
You will have noticed another column on the Condition list screen which is
filled with 0s. This column is where you put the score that the player gets
for doing something. So, if you want them to get 2 points for looking at
the signpost, scroll down to "Look at type 1", press S and type 2. They will
only get the points the first time the event occurs. The maximum number
of points per event is 120 (why would you give someone that many?).
Note that you can take points off by entering a negative number.
2.5.2 THE SCRIPT EDITOR
New in v1.00 SR-1 of the Adventure Creator is a graphical script editor. This
allows you to create more advanced reactions to events than was possible
before. It also eliminates the need for misc conditions (if you don't know
what they are, see 2.5.3 below). Notice how in the demo game, when you talk
to the man by the door he now reacts differently if you have already spoken to
him. This is made possible by the script editor.
Suppose that when the player clicks the hand on a bottle (object 0), you want
to display a message and remove the object. To do this, set the reaction for
"Action on object 0" to "Run graphical script". Then, hit F10 to launch the
script editor.
The current script is shown on the left hand side of the screen. All the
possible actions are listed on the right hand side. To add an action to the
script, drag the icon from the right hand side to the position you want to
insert it on the left. To delete an action, drag it back into the list on the
right.
In our example, drag "Remove object %d" from the right hand side to the left
hand side of the screen. A dialog will appear asking you to type in the
replacement for %d. Since the bottle is object 0, type in 0 and press return.
Now, drag "Display message %d" from the right to below "remove object" on the
left. (dragging it *below* means it happens afterwards. In this case, it
doesn't matter which way round the actions occur, but usually it would).
You can change the settings for a script line by double-clicking on it.

IMPORTANT: The run-time engine DOES NOT currently support the following actions
when used in a graphical script. Using them will cause an error:
 - Go to screen %d
 - Remove obj %d, add inv
 - Add object %d to inventory
 - Run text script %d
If you need to use these reactions, you must use the old misc condition method.
I hope to implement these by the next release.
IMPORTANT: Actions such as giving score and adding object to inventory *will*
be repeated in a graphical script (in a normal response these will only be
activated the first time the event happens). Thus to make sure the player
can only score the points once, you should use flags with conditional
statements, as explained below.

2.5.2.1 CONDITIONAL STATEMENTS
The main benefit of the script editor is that it allows you to create
conditional responses - that is, the response is different depending on what
has happened previously. You do this through the use of FLAGS. A flag is
a variable which contains either 'true' or 'false'.
In the script editor, each flag has a name. You name the flags, giving them
descriptive names of what they do. Let's look at an example. Say you have a
man on the screen. The first time the player talks to him, he should explain
the objective of the game. However, if the player then talks to him again,
he will just reply with something like "I've told you what to do, now get on
with it".
Drag 'If flag %s is clear' onto the left. Either choose a flag from the list
or click "New Flag..." to create a new one. Then, double click the "display
message %d" which it automatically creates and type a message number. Now,
drag 'Set flag %s' onto 'display message'. Choose the same flag you used
earlier. This means that the next time the script is called, the flag will be
set and so the message will not be displayed.
Drag 'Stop Script' onto the 'display message'. You may need to swap them
round (so that Stop Script happens *after* displaying the message). Stop
script tells the game to immediately abort the current graphical script.
Now, drag another 'display message' from the right to the bottom of the
script, after the 'stop script' line. The result should look like this:
 If flag TALKED is clear
   Set flag TALKED
   Display message 1
   Stop script
 Display message 2
So, the first time you talk to him message 1 will be displayed, then after
that message 2 will be displayed. Press ESCape to return to the condition list.
NOTE: Each screen has a different set of flags, so any flags you use on one
screen cannot be used on another.

2.5.3 USING MISCELLANEOUS CONDITIONS
Before the script editor was written, you could have multiple responses to
an event by using misc conditions. These are obsolete now, but are still
supported for backward compatibility. Another way of doing the example
in section 2.5.2 would be:
Set the reaction for "Action on object 0" to "Remove object %d", and type 0.
Now, press M and type in 1. Scroll the list to "Misc condition 1" and set the
reaction to "Display Message %d". You can chain as many misc conditions as you
like this way.

Some reactions require the use of another 0-filled column, the Data column.
See the command reference and on-line help for more info.

2.6 SCREEN EDGES
The last thing you have to do is to define the edges of the screen. For
example, you might want a door which leads to another screen or you might
want to lower the top edge. Click on  Condition | Edges  . The defaults will
be displayed. Use the RIGHT button to cycle through to the edge you want
to alter and then use the LEFT button to change it.
You will probably want the game to change screens when the character walks
off the edge. To do this, choose  Condition | Change  and move the + to
"Character walks off right". Press RETURN and select "Go to screen %d" from
the menu. Use BACKSPACE to erase the 0 and type 2. So now when the character
walks off the right hand edge, ROOM2.CRM will be loaded and the character
will be displayed on the left hand side. Of course, you will have to also
change screen 2 so that when you walk off the left you go back to screen 1.
It is quite useful to draw a map on a piece of paper of the way the screens
link up or you could have a nightmare trying to remember which screen is on
the left of screen 8, etc.

2.7 OBJECTS
There are some things which are not required but it is sometimes necessary
to use. These are objects, which can change, move and disappear - something
which the background scene can't. For example, suppose you want a key to
be lying on the floor. If you drew it on the background scene there'd be
no way to erase it when it was taken. With an object, however, this is
possible. There is a limit of 10 objects per screen.
To add an object choose  Misc | Objects  . Click the LEFT button to position
the object, press N to change what it looks like or press A to cycle
through the objects. The current object number (0-9) is displayed on the
title bar. When you've finished, press ESC.
There is a selection of 20 pictures for objects. Most of them aren't much
good and so you can say they're whatever you like. If you want to use your
own objects, send them to me in 256-color PCX or BMP format and I'll
convert them and supply them as an add-on package (registered users only).
To define what happens when the player interacts with the objects, click
on  Condition | Change  and use the "Look at object 0" through to
"Speak to object 9". For example, if you want a key to disappear when taken,
select "Action on object 0" and choose "Remove object %d" and type 0.
When objects are displayed during the game, you will be drawn behind them if
you are in that position: you don't need walk-behind areas on objects.

2.8 TESTING YOUR GAME
To finish off your game and make it playable you need to create a project.
A project is basically the name that your game will be known by in DOS (usual
rules apply: 8 chars max, although a Win95 version will probably come soon).
Each project must have 2 files: xxxx.INI and xxxx.SCR. The .INI file is the
General Instruction File, the .SCR file is the script file (more advanced
users only). If you aren't using scripting, the file still needs to be there
but can be empty.
In this example, I will create a project called MYGAME. Replace MYGAME with
whatever you're calling your project. First of all, copy the TEMPLATE.INI file
to MYGAME.INI and the TEMPLATE.SCR file to MYGAME.SCR. Now you would modify
the MYGAME.INI file to suit your game (see section 3.4), but to begin with
it's probably best to just leave it as it is and try out the game.
Once you have the .INI and .SCR files you need to compile the project, which
will produce a single file MYGAME.PRG, the file you will distribute with your
game. To compile your project, either type
AC MYGAME /COMPILE
or choose  Action | Compile  from the Project Manager.
Assuming there are no errors the file MYGAME.PRG will be produced, otherwise
you will be notified and must correct the errors then try again.
If all went well, to run the game just type
AC MYGAME
or choose  Action | Run Game  from the Project Manager, and the game should
load and place you in the first room.

3. Other features
-----------------
3.1 GLOBAL MESSAGES
In the tutorial I explained how to create a message to be displayed when
an event happened. Suppose, however, that you have pictures of trees on more
than one screen. Obviously, you don't want to have to enter the message on
each screen. For this purpose there are GLOBAL MESSAGES: these can be
displayed from any screen. You place the global messages in the GIF. Global
message numbering starts at 500 so as not to confuse the messages with the
individual room messages. So, for example, a GIF line might read:
Message 500 = I think it's a tree. It looks like one.
You would set the condition for looking at the tree to "Display message 500".
More about GIFs later.

3.2 MUSIC
Note: The new DPMI version of the AC doesn't support music. Support will be
added soon; however for now please ignore the following section:
Something which will give your game more appeal is sound. The Adventure
Creator currently supports the Sound Blaster and PC squeaker, although
squeaker support is probably not necessary any more as the industry is
changing so rapidly. The music used has the file name MUSIC*.CMF for the Sound
Blaster and MUSIC*.MUS for the beeper. The included MUSMAKE program will
allow you to create MUS beeper music, and shareware & commercial programs
will allow you to create CMF files. Also included is MID2CMF which can convert
some MIDI files to the CMF format.
When the game loads MUSIC0.CMF (or MUSIC0.MUS) will be played. If you want
a specific tune to be played when the player enters a screen, load that
screen in ROOMMAKE and choose  Misc | Options  . Click on the word NONE
alongside "Start music" until the correct number appears. For example, to
play MUSIC15.CMF when the player enters the screen, keep clicking until
the number 15 appears.
The game automatically detects if a SoundBlaster is installed and uses it,
otherwise the beeper MUS music will be used.

3.3 USING PASSWORDS
You will probably want to give your files some security to stop other users
of the Adventure Creator from looking at (and changing) them. For this reason,
RoomMake provides the  Misc | Password  option. Before you save the screen,
choose this and click in the box. Type in a password of up to 10 letters (the
password is case-sensitive: hello and Hello are different). In future when
you want to edit the screen then before you click on  File | Load room...
you have to click on  Misc | Password  and type in the password. If you
attempt to load a screen you don't know the password to then a message will
inform you and the screen won't load. To change a password just load the
screen, change the password with  Misc | Password  and save the screen.
NOTE: The password is only used in ROOMMAKE. The main game engine bypasses
the password (otherwise people wouldn't be able to play your game!).
NOTE: There is an additional layer of security you can use, building a data
file. See section 3.6 for details.

3.4 THE GENERAL INSTRUCTION FILE
Late Note: The editing of the GIF is now made much easier by the introduction
of the Project Manager; please see section 2.0 for details. If you want to
edit it the hard way, read on...
The General Instruction File (GIF): This file contains information about
global things (they don't change from screen to screen). Its name is
????.INI, where the ???? represents a name of your choosing. Note that if
you want to have a script file, that must have the same name (but with .SCR
extension). The layout of the GIF is as follows:

Command[ number] = EqualTo<Ù

The carriage return defines the end of the command.
There are several commands you can use, the most common of which is probably
the MESSAGE command. This defines a global message (see section 3.1). This
would be set out like:

Message 501 = It's a rock. They are small grey things that aren't much use.

There are 4 lines that must be present in the GIF file. These are Messages
997 - 999 and TotalScore. Message 999 is displayed if the player clicks the
eye on the character, Message 998 if they click the hand and 997 if they
talk to themselves. TotalScore is the 10 in "Score: 0 of 10". See section 3.5
for how to work this out. The defaults for the 4 compulsory commands are:

Message 997 = Talking to yourself is a sign of madness!
Message 998 = Keep your hands off yourself!
Message 999 = Damn, I'm looking good!
TotalScore = 10

A list of all GIF commands is in the Command Reference (section 4.1).
If you modify the GIF file you must re-compile the project to see the changes
in the game: see section 2.8 for information.

3.5 CALCULATING THE TOTAL SCORE
To work this out you can either go through adding all the points up manually
or get the Adventure Creator to do it for you. Just type ADVEN ROOM /SCORE,
or choose  Action | Total Score  from the Project Manager, and it will add up
the total number of points possible. It does this by checking all ROOM*.CRM
files so if you have a screen with a different name it won't be included in
the total.
Note that negative scores will NOT be subtracted from the total, so if it
isn't possible to avoid losing 5 points for doing something, the total score
will be too high. Also, any modifications to the score within the script file
will not be counted.

3.6 BUILDING A DATA FILE
The problem with having all the necessary files lying around the directory
is that it's harder to find what you want and that other people can view
the files. If you're going to be distributing a game, you don't want other
people looking at your artwork, sound, etc without playing the game. OK, so
the .CRM files do have password protection but the WIN.PCX, LOSE.PCX,
ADVEN.INI and sound files don't. To overcome this problem, you can build a
data file. For this purpose a program called MAKEDATA is included. It will
create a file containing all the above files (*.CRM, *.CMF, *.MUS, *.PCX
and xxx.PRG). This will be called ACGAME.DAT and can be distributed instead
of all the seperate files. This, as well as being more secure, makes
distribution easier.
Only use a data file for distribution - if you use it normally then
you will have to keep re-building it whenever you alter a file.

3.7 SCRIPTING
Scripting adds much more power to the Adventure Creator for more experienced/
computer literate people. If you have some programming experience then you
will find scripting easy to master, if not then it won't take long to pick up.
Basically, scripting provides you with the ability to manipulate variables,
for example the player's score, and to run several conditions without having
to chain them as misc conditions. The script file has the extension xxxx.SCR,
where xxxx is your project name (see section 2.8). Scripts are written in
a C-like language, but it is very limited at present.
3.7.1 MACROS
Macros are ways to easily remember a particular number. You define a macro
using the "define" keyword, like follows:
define SHOWMESSAGE 5
This means that now, whenever you use the word SHOWMESSAGE in the script,
the compiler will replace it with the number 5. Macros make it easier to
understand the script that you've written than just a jumble of numbers. Also,
in the future if the Display Message function was changed to number 11, all
you would need to do to the script would be to change the line to read
define SHOWMESSAGE 11
instead of changing every reference. You can have a maximum of 40 macros
per project.
3.7.2 VARIABLES
There are two types of variables available in scripting: built-in and user
defined. The built-in variables are things like the player's score, which
relates directly to the game and if modified will update the game immediately.
The user defined variables are what you use to keep track of things like
whether the player has been to a certain location. User defined variables must
be declared at the beginning of the script file, like follows:
variable been_there, got_torch, hungry;
variable example, test;
The above lines would define five variables, "been_there", "got_torch",
"hungry", "example" and "test". There is no limit to the number of variables
per line nor to the number of lines of variables, but you can only have a
maximum of 20 user-defined variables per project. All user-defined variables
are set to 0 at the beginning of the game.
3.7.3 THE PROGRAM
The program is written in blocks, each with a different number. When a
condition in a room is run which says "run script 6", for example, block
number 6 will run.
A block is started by the line
script x {
where x is the block number, and finished by a
}
on its own on a line.
In between these two lines goes the actual program. Variables are modified
using the supported operators. A list of these is below:
=   assignment
-=  decrement
+=  increment
|=  bitwise OR
Comparisons are made using the "IF" keyword and an operator from the following
list. Note that at present AND and OR aren't supported, so you can only have
one comparison per line.
==  is equal to
!=  is not equal to
<   is less than
>   is more than
An example of a complete script follows:
script 1 {
  if (got_torch==1) {                checks if user variable got_torch is 1
    player.score+=10                 adds 10 to the player's score
    got_torch=2                      sets got_torch to 2
    }
  }
Comments can be added by placing a semicolon ";" or double slash "//" at the
start of the line: any line beginning with one of these is ignored.


4. Command reference
--------------------
4.1 THE GENERAL INSTRUCTION FILE
The GIF contains many additional optional commands not described above. Below
is a list of all valid GIF commands:

þ GameName = name
    Defines the text displayed on the right hand side of the title bar,
    usually the game name. If not supplied, defaults to Chris Quest!
þ Inventory number = name
    Defines what the inventory item will be called when the player presses
    TAB to view inventory.
þ Message message_number = message_text
    The MESSAGE command defines a message to be displayed with the "Display
    Message %d" option in the Condition dialogue. Messages 997-999 have
    special meaning (see section 3.4), and messages 0-100 are different in
    different rooms. Messages 500-990 are defined with the MESSAGE command.
    These are global messages, which can be displayed from any room.
þ NearObjects = Yes | No
    Whether you need to be near an object to pick it up or speak to it.
    Default=Yes. If set to no, can take an object from anywhere on screen.
þ NightTime = Yes | No
    Whether the game has time, default=No. If Yes, then pressing Ctrl-T will
    display the current time. At 19:00 the screen will darken, lighting up
    again at 08:00 the next day.
þ ScoreSound = Yes | No
    Whether a sound is played when the player does something which adds
    points to their score. Default=Yes.
þ ScoreTick = Yes | No
    Defines whether when you recieve, for example, 6 points, the score just
    changes (ScoreTick=No) or whether it does a LSL3-type adding on the
    points 1 by 1 (ScoreTick=Yes).
þ StartPosX = x
þ StartPosY = y
    Defines the position of the character on the first screen when the game
    starts. The values are in the range 1-300 for X, and 30-195 for Y.
    The default position is (100,100), which is in the left middle of the
    screen.
þ TotalScore = score
    Defines the total score possible, displayed in the game in the title bar
    as "Score: 0 of 20". To work it out, use ADVEN /SCORE (see section 3.5).

4.2 RESPONSES TO EVENTS
Event responses are edited by using  Condition | Change . Events occur when
the player walks off the screen, looks at something, talks to something
or interacts with something. There are also 20 miscellaneous events which
can be used to produce more than one reaction to an event (see section 2.5.3).
Below are the possible reactions and what they do:

þ Go to screen %d
    If this is set for one of the first 4 conditions (walking off the edges)
    then the character will first walk all the way off the screen.
    The new screen will be loaded and the character placed on the opposite
    side, unless the Data column is set. This value defines which side of
    the screen to place the character (1000=left, 2000=right, 3000=bottom,
    4000=top). The character will be placed in the middle of this side, unless
    the pixel offset is added (0-319 for top & bottom, 0-199 for left and
    right). For example, Data=2150 will place the man near the bottom of the
    right hand side. The Data value is mandatory if the condition is not one
    of the first four.
þ No action
    Ignores the event.
þ Blocked: Can't walk
    Prevents the character from entering the condition area or object.
þ Character dies
    The game ends, displaying LOSE.PCX. Optionally, the Data column can
    contain a number (1-99) which will display LOSE??.PCX instead. This way
    you can have different endings depending on how they die.
þ Run animation %d
    [This response is not yet implemented]
þ Display Message %d
    Displays the message defined with  Condition | Messages .
þ Remove object %d
    Removes the defined object from the game. It will no longer appear on
    this screen.
þ Remove obj %d, add inv
    Removes the object from the game, and adds inventory number Data to
    the inventory. For example, if the GIF contains "Inventory 3 = Cup" then
    entering 3 in the Data column will add "Cup" to the inventory list.
þ Add inventory %d
    Adds the inventory number to the inventory. Data column defines how
    many times this may be done. For example, Data=2 means that the first
    two times the event occurs, inv will be added. Will only chain to misc
    conditions if adding inv, so chaining to a misc condition which describes
    what's happening would be OK.
þ Run script %d
    Runs a script (see section 3.7 for details).

4.3 CONDITIONS
Below is a list of all the conditions on the  Condition | Change  screen,
and how they are triggered:

þ Character walks off xxxxxx
    Occurs when the man walks off the edge of the screen (Condition | Edges).
þ Player first enters screen
    Occurs the first time the player enters the screen. Useful for showing
    a message describing the scene.
þ Character stands on type xx
    Occurs when the man walks onto condition area xx.
þ Look at type xx
    Occurs when the eye cursor is clicked on condition area xx.
þ Action on type xx
    Occurs when the hand cursor is clicked on condition area xx.
þ Use inv on type xx
    Occurs when the bag cursor is clicked on condition area xx. Data must
    contain the number of the inventory item which will trigger the condition.
    Therefore to use a reaction which needs the Data column you must choose
    "No action" and use a Misc Condition.
þ Look at object xx
    Occurs when the eye cursor is clicked on object xx.
þ Action on object xx
    Occurs when the hand cursor is clicked on object xx.
þ Talk to object xx
    Occurs when the head cursor is clicked on object xx.
þ Use inv on object xx
    Occurs when the bag cursor is clicked on object xx. See description for
    "Use inv on type xx" for more info.
þ Misc condition xx
    Occurs after the condition which triggered it.

4.4 SCRIPTING BUILT-INS
The following variables are pre-defined and may be used in your program:
 player.score    The player's current score
*player.x        The present X co-ordinate of the man's feet (from 0-319)
*player.y        The present Y co-ordinate of the man's feet (from 0-200)
 inv[1]          Whether you have inventory number 1    and so on, up to...
 inv[46]         Whether you have inventory number 46
Those marked with an asterisk * are read-only variables. The compiler does
not enforce this rule, so it will allow you to change the variables. However,
doing so can cause problems and conflicting results in the game.
The following functions are built in and may be called by your program:
runcondition(response,value,data)
  Runs a response as if it had occured by a condition being met. response is
  the type, for example 0 means go to screen and 5 means display message.
  value is the equivalent of the "%d" value in ROOMMAKE. For example, value=3
  and response=0 means go to screen 3. data is the equivalent of the data
  column in ROOMMAKE. Note that no score or misc columns are needed since
  these are implemented in other ways in scripting. Example of use:
  runcondition(7,1,4)   This would remove object 1 and add inventory number 4.

5. Troubleshooting
------------------

If you encounter problems, please check this section first before contacting
me for help. If you don't find the solution here, please read section 6.3
first before e-mailing me.

5.1 GENERAL PROBLEMS
This section deals with problems encountered in both ROOMMAKE and AC.

Q. I keep getting "Out of memory" errors: how can I free more up?
A. Both RoomMake and AC have now been ported to DPMI, which means there
   should be no memory problems unless you only have 2 Mb RAM. If you do,
   all I can say is upgrade!
   If you do get an out of memory error, contact me as it should never
   happen any more.

Q. After changing a condition in ROOMMAKE, when I return to the main screen
   there is a fuzz near the bottom of the screen.
A. This was a bug in some beta versions of the program. Contact Chris Jones
   for an update.

Q. When playing the game, I get an 'Unhandled exception' and a load of
   technogarbage in big print and it crashes.
A. If this happens to you, note down the details and mail me. The only known
   cause of this was the old version of the game driver. It may also happen
   in revision 10.07.97.A or earlier if you place an object on the screen
   which uses the new graphics (the pictures from the radio through to the
   piece of parchment). If this is the case, you should upgrade your version,
   check the website (see section 6.3) for details.

Q. AC says a mouse driver is not installed, but it is.
A. Check that your mouse driver is Microsoft-compatible. This error is also
   caused by running some incompatible DPMI hosts, for example Borland 32RTM
   in the background. If this happens, try running from a Windows DOS box,
   and see if that cures the problem.

Q. When I try to compile my game, AC says "(E202): General instruction file
   incomplete" and exits.
A. You have not included a required line in the .INI file. Check that
   you have the TotalScore and GameName lines, and that there is a space
   each side of the = sign.

Q. In ROOMMAKE, I create a message and type it in, and the program quits
   displaying "MYALLOC (E182): Memory corrupt". What can I do?
A. This is a bug which seems to occur when you've used File | Start New, or if
   you've loaded too many different rooms.
    - To start a new room, quit and then load Roommake again.
    - To modify an existing room, quit then re-load Roommake and load this
      room straight away.

Q. When I try to compile my game, it says "(E211): Game driver not found".
A. This means that the ACDRV.DLL file was not found in the current working
   directory. Make sure that the working directory is the same directory as
   that containing the ADVEN.EXE and ACDRV.DLL files. If you have copied some
   of the Adventure Creator out of its original directory, you must copy
   ADVEN.EXE, AC.BAT, ACDRV.DLL and AC.DAT. If you leave one of these files
   out, you may get strange error messages.

5.2 GAME ERRORS
Below is a list of error messages and their meanings:
NOTE: Most of the errors are to do with the Data column. If you think you
have corrected the problem, make sure you aren't loading a save game which
you saved before updating the screen. (save games include the room setups).

Attempted to display non-existent message
   You have a response such as "Display message 542" but message 542 doesn't
   exist.
Found lonely condition 7 (REMOVE & ADD)
   You haven't specified which inventory item to add (Data column reads 0).
Found lonely UseInvOnObject condition
Found lonely UseInvOnType condition
   You have not specified which inventory item needs to be used to cause
   the condition (Data column reads 0).
Go_to_screen used without Data column
   You have used Go To Screen not as a response to one of the first four
   conditions, therefore the Data column is mandatory. See section 4.2.
Run_graph_script: unknown event XX
   You used an invalid response in the graphical script. This is explained
   in section 2.5.2.

6. Miscellaneous
----------------
I think I've spelt that word right...

6.1 DISTRIBUTING YOUR GAMES
There are several ways of marketing your games. You could distribute a
"shareware version" which only has the first 10 rooms, and then ask for
registration for the complete game. Or, you could make a short game showing
off your abilities and ask people to buy a full game.
Under the new swapware license, you may distribute your games in and way
and even *sell* them as long as you send me a copy of your game.
6.1.1 Which files do I need?
For the game to run properly, when distributing your game you must include:
RTM.EXE, ADVEN.EXE, ACDRV.DLL, AC.DAT, AC.BAT, ROOM*.CRM (your screens),
xxx.PRG, MUSIC*.CMF, MUSIC*.MUS, LOSE*.PCX and WIN.PCX.
Or, if you have built a data file, you just need RTM.EXE, ADVEN.EXE, AC.DAT,
AC.BAT, ACDRV.DLL and your data file.
You MUST NOT include the following files: ROOMMAKE.EXE, ROOMMAKE.HLP,
ROOMMAKE.CLB and AC.DOC (this file). These files are not necessary for the
game to run and should NOT be included.

6.2 NOTE FROM THE AUTHOR
This is the first release of the Adventure Creator. Any bug notices, comments
and suggestions would be appreciated in order to improve future versions.
Also, I can't wait to see what you can come up with! As I'm not much of an
artist, the demo game is NOT very impressive. So, to promote interest in the
Adventure Creator, send your games to me and the best will be distributed
with the program (free marketing for your product).

6.3 CONTACTING THE AUTHOR
If you have any comments/suggestions, mail me at  dosuser@hotmail.com
If you think you've found a bug, if you need help or can't get it to work,
please check the following:
 * That you are using the most recent version of the Creator. You can
   find out the most recent version by checking the website (address below).
   You can find out your version by typing  AC /?  and noting the line that
   reads "Adventure Creator vx.xx" (x.xx = version number), and the line which
   reads "Revision DD.MM.YY.R" (date/month/year/sub-version).
 * If you are receiving an error message, please write down the EXACT message
   and any other information which you think is appropriate. PLEASE WRITE THE
   EXACT ERROR MESSAGE TEXT, don't just say "I get a message about it not being
   able to find a file".
 * If you are getting lock-ups or techno-garbage printouts, please try to
   give a situation which causes the problem. For instance, if you notice that
   it only happens on certain screens, or only if there is an object, etc then
   please state that - it makes replying a lot easier and faster.
Please note: I usually check my e-mail two or three times a week, so give me
about a week to reply before assuming your message didn't get through.
The AC website:  http://members.xoom.com/dosuser/ac.htm

6.4 APPEAL
I really want to upgrade the Creator to 32-bit DPMI, probably DJGPP. This
would enable music and sound to be added, as well as speed improvements.
However, I don't know of a good dynamic linking system for DJGPP and I don't
want to incorporate the ACDRV.DLL driver into the EXE file. If you know of
a free dynamic linker, let me know!


7. Technical Info
-----------------

This section might be of interest to the technically-minded amongst you, or
maybe it isn't. But I'll include it anyway:

7.1 SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS: WHY?
Below I list the sys reqs and explain why they are required:
 - An 80386 or above processor
     This is required because the run-time engine uses DPMI (DOS Protected-
     Mode Interface), which will only run on a 386 processor. DPMI is what
     allows it to use extended memory and thus not requiring you to have
     lots of conventional memory free.
 - A mouse with driver loaded
     A mouse is required because it is by far the easiest way to play the
     game, and I couldn't be bothered to write a substitute keyboard interface
     for if you don't have a mouse.
     A driver is required because that's how DOS applications interface with
     the mouse. If running in a shell from Windows (3.x or 95), a driver is
     not required (because Windows provides one).
 - A VGA card & monitor
     These days everyone has a VGA monitor, so it's hardly a big deal. The
     game uses VGA 320x200 in 256 colours because it's the fastest graphics
     mode available. I did consider SVGA, but that would increase memory
     requirements and lower performance too much to make it worthwhile.
 - 2 Mb RAM
     All I can say about this is: that's how much memory the programs use.

7.2 WHAT IS ALL THIS ABOUT DPMI?
In the beginning, PCs were designed to cope with a maximum of 640k of memory.
At the time, that amount sounded massive, and PCs weren't expected to last
more than a few years, so there was no problem.
But PCs became more and more popular, and advanced, so that soon 640k was
a constraint. Different systems were thought up: first EMS, then XMS, which
could both access more than 640k of memory. However, these techniques
required copying data between them and conventional memory, thus causing
the program to run slower.
With the invention of the 80286 came it's ability to run in Protected-Mode,
which allowed programs to access up to 16 Mb of RAM. However, the CPU normally
remained in Real Mode for compatibility with older programs. Only special
programs, called DOS extenders, could switch the processor to protected mode.
The first major DOS extender was Microsoft Windows 3.0, which could access
up to 16 Mb of RAM.
The invention of the 80386 brought 32-bit Protected-Mode, which meant programs
could access up to 4 Gb of RAM, however this mode required a different type
of DOS extender. The 80386 also enhanced the 16-bit (286) Protected-Mode, by
adding the feature of enabling it to use virtual memory: using free disk
space to simulate memory. This was introduced in Windows 3.1 when running
in "386 enhanced mode".
By this time there were other DOS extenders coming on the market, mainly
designed at programmers writing DOS games, since most other programs had
switched to Windows. The 16-bit extenders included Phar Lap 286 and Borland's
RTM (which is what the Adventure Creator uses).
Recently 32-bit extenders have become more popular. Examples of these include
Windows 95, CWSDPMI (used by ROOMMAKE), DOS/4GW, and 32RTM.

